QUESTIONS=[
 'Translate to parallel axes through (a) (0,2): 2x−y+2=0; (b) (−1,2): x²+y²+2x−4y+1=0; (c) (3,4): x²+2y²−6x+16y+39=0; (d) (−2,2): x²+y²−3xy+10x−10y+21=0.',
 'Transform to axes inclined (a) 45°: x²−y²=a²; (b) 90°: y²=4px; (c) 45°: x²+y²+4xy−1=0; (d) 45°: x²−y²−2√2x−10√2y+2=0.',
 'Transform to new axes inclined (a) tan⁻¹(1/2), through (1,−2): 14x²+11y²−36x+48y−4xy+41=0; (b) tan⁻¹(−4/3), through (2,−1): 11x²+4y²−20x−40y+24xy−5=0; (c) tan⁻¹(3/4), through (3,1): 3x²+10y²+6x+52y−24xy=0.',
 'Find an angle for which the transformed equation has no XY term: (a) 11x²+4y²−20x−40y+24xy−5=0; (b) 5x²+7y²+2√3xy−16=0.'
]
STEPS=[
 ['For a new origin (h,k), substitute x=X+h and y=Y+k while keeping the axes parallel.','(a) x=X,y=Y+2 gives 2X − (Y+2) + 2 = 0, hence 2X−Y=0.','(b) x=X−1,y=Y+2 gives (X−1)²+(Y+2)²+2(X−1)−4(Y+2)+1=0. Linear terms cancel, yielding X²+Y²−4=0.','(c) x=X+3,y=Y+4 gives X²+2Y²+32Y+126=0. The linear X term cancels; the Y coefficient is 4·4+16=32.','The source asks translation through (3,4), so retain this equation. Completing its Y square gives X²+2(Y+8)²=2; the original conic center is (3,−4), a different point.','(d) x=X−2,y=Y+2 cancels both linear terms and leaves X²+Y²−3XY+1=0. Reinsert inverse coordinates X=x−h,Y=y−k in each answer to recover the original equations.'],
 ['Use x=Xcosθ−Ysinθ,y=Xsinθ+Ycosθ. For θ=45°, x=(X−Y)/√2,y=(X+Y)/√2.','(a) x²−y² = ((X−Y)²−(X+Y)²)/2 = −2XY, hence −2XY=a² or XY=−a²/2.','(b) At 90°, x=−Y,y=X. Thus X²=−4pY. The rotation formulas apply at 90° as well.','(c) x²+y²=X²+Y² and 4xy=2(X²−Y²), giving 3X²−Y²−1=0.','(d) The squared part becomes −2XY. The linear terms become −2(X−Y)−10(X+Y)=−12X−8Y.','Hence −2XY−12X−8Y+2=0, or XY+6X+4Y−1=0. These transformations retain the same curves in new coordinates.'],
 ['Use x=h+Xcosθ−Ysinθ,y=k+Xsinθ+Ycosθ. First translate the center, then expand the rotated quadratic.','(a) Let u=x−1,v=y+2. The equation becomes 14u²−4uv+11v²−25=0. For tanθ=1/2, cosθ=2/√5,sinθ=1/√5.','Substitute u=(2X−Y)/√5,v=(X+2Y)/√5, yielding (59/5)X²−(24/5)XY+(66/5)Y²−25=0, or 59X²−24XY+66Y²−125=0. The requested positive angle leaves an XY term.','(b) Let u=x−2,v=y+1: 11u²+24uv+4v²−5=0. Using the principal angle tan⁻¹(−4/3), cosθ=3/5,sinθ=−4/5.','Thus u=(3X+4Y)/5,v=(−4X+3Y)/5 gives −5X²+20Y²−5=0, or 4Y²−X²=1.','(c) Let u=x−3,v=y−1: 3u²−24uv+10v²+35=0. With cosθ=4/5,sinθ=3/5, substitute u=(4X−3Y)/5,v=(3X+4Y)/5.','The result is −6X²+19Y²+35=0, or 6X²−19Y²=35. Exact expansion checks every coefficient, including the constants at the new origins.'],
 ['For Ax²+Bxy+Cy², the mixed coefficient after rotation is (C−A)sin2θ+Bcos2θ. Set it to zero.','(a) A=11,B=24,C=4 gives tan2θ=24/7.','One solution is θ=tan⁻¹(3/4)≈36.86989765°, since tan2θ=2(3/4)/(1−9/16)=24/7. Angles differing by 90° also work.','(b) A=5,B=2√3,C=7 gives tan2θ=−√3.','Thus θ=−30° or 60° modulo 90°. The positive counterclockwise choice 60° gives 8X²+4Y²−16=0.','At −30° the squared coefficients are interchanged: 4X²+8Y²−16=0. Both remove XY; the division-free coefficient condition verifies the angle branches.']
]
ANSWERS=['(a) 2X−Y=0; (b) X²+Y²=4; (c) X²+2Y²+32Y+126=0; (d) X²+Y²−3XY+1=0.','(a) XY=−a²/2; (b) X²=−4pY; (c) 3X²−Y²=1; (d) XY+6X+4Y−1=0.','(a) 59X²−24XY+66Y²−125=0; (b) 4Y²−X²=1; (c) 6X²−19Y²=35.','(a) θ≈36.86989765°; (b) θ=60° or −30°, modulo 90°.']
PAGES=[page('146_page_1.jpg',283,[heading('Solution — Example 31'),paragraph('If the axes are rotated through θ, find the angle through result (102):'),eq(r'\theta=\frac12\tan^{-1}\frac{2h}{a-b},\quad a=9,\ b=4,\ h=6'),eq(r'\theta=\frac12\tan^{-1}\frac{2(6)}{9-4}=\frac12\tan^{-1}\frac{12}{5}=\frac12\tan^{-1}(2.4)=\frac12(67^\circ)\approx34^\circ'),heading('Exercise 9.4')]+[paragraph(str(i+1)+'. '+q) for i,q in enumerate(QUESTIONS)]+[note('Example 31’s exact angle is tan⁻¹(2/3)≈33.69006753°, equivalent to half tan⁻¹(12/5); source 34° is a rounded value. In Question 3(a), the prescribed positive angle does not diagonalize the conic; the worked answer correctly retains XY. In Question 4(b), angle −30° or 60° removes XY; the latter meets a positive counterclockwise convention.')])]
